First thing we're going to do is reproduce and clean up the preprocessing
pipeline. The current one is janky and has no tests or anything to keep it out
of trouble.

Here's what I think happens:

- Certain image data layers are rendered as tiles for dynamic fetching. This includes the WAC, Sectionals, TACs, IFR High & Low, a few specialty charts (like the
Portland inset and the Grand Canyon). I assume these all use the standard
mercator quadtree. (whenever I make assumptions, correct me if they're wrong!)

- Other image data are small enough that they're standalone, like approach plates,
af/d ("chart supplement" as the kids call them these days). Some of these
have georeferencing data, but rather than squish the chart to a standard geo
layout, the client goes the opposite direction from the tiling approach: it
renders the chart roughly in its original pixels, and draws a little airplane
by mapping geo coordinates to plate coordinates.
    - Some of the georeference data is collected by the Avare folks. Can we
    fetch it from a database they provide?


We're going to write our preprocessor in Rust.
It will have an intelligent fetching-and-caching layer so we're not hitting the
upstream image sources over-and-over during testing.
It will organize its work in a way that reveals a lot of parallelism, since the
tile rendering task is pretty parallelizable. We want to get a lot of benefit
from the 20 cores on my server.

One thing we should do is run a copy of the original preprocessor and collect
logs of all the tiles it made, and figure out how to use that info to write
tests to ensure that our version doesn't introduce new gaps.

We can do some quick prototypes here, but once we really start chewing through
data, we'll move that work onto a container on my server to exploit its many
cores. If you think we can exploit a GPU, I can give you one of those too.

I think our first cut should reproduce the layout & data format of the original
tool directly, so we can compare their output straight across and confirm
that the tiles match: all the same tiles selected, and sample a bunch to confirm
they basically match visually.

I understand the original code is a hodge-podge of scripts. I'd like our thing
to be as clean and orderly as possible for maintenance going forward.

Considering what you've read already and the goals I've stated here, make a
plan and discuss.
